00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.287
And I want to start
by framing this.

00:02:08.287 --> 00:02:10.620
We ask these key cost-management
questions in every talk

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:11.070
that we're giving.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.320
And I think the nice things
about these questions are,

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.330
they're evergreen,
they're fundamental,

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.790
they apply whether or not
you're running in the cloud,

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.750
running on premises,
running a hybrid solution.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.250
They apply whether or not you're
running pure infrastructure

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.980
or you're doing something really
cool with Kubernetes or Istio.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.999
So first, obviously, how
much does my app cost?

00:02:27.999 --> 00:02:29.040
You're building your app.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.520
You want to make sure that what
it costs is less than the value

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.770
it's delivering to you.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.050
And you can answer this
at many different levels.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.570
You could just say, in general,
am I spending less than X

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:40.380
per month?

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.910
Or you could ask
yourself, how much

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.410
is this VM that's been running
for a long time costing me,

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:46.337
and is it worth keeping
that thing running?

00:02:46.337 --> 00:02:48.795
The second question is, what
are the trends in those costs?

00:02:48.795 --> 00:02:51.210
So are the costs
going up as expected,

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.190
or are they going
up unexpectedly?

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.200
Is the trend good or bad?

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.930
Growing costs are
not a bad thing.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:59.040
Your costs could grow
with customer demand.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.330
And if they're growing
sublinearly with customer

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:02.460
demand, that's great.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:03.570
That's a great growth.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:06.360
Or it grew unexpectedly
because someone

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.510
did a really fancy analysis
they didn't need to on BigQuery,

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.190
and they got an
answer that might not

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.077
have been worth the cost.

00:03:13.077 --> 00:03:14.910
And then finally, what
are the cost drivers?

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:18.310
So what is it that's
driving the costs?

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:20.040
Do you understand them?

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.820
Are you able to
delegate cost analysis

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.220
to the right people in your
organization to understand

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:27.720
the costs at the right level?

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:32.319
And that's a lot about what
this talk is going to be about.

00:03:32.319 --> 00:03:34.110
So before we dive in
a little bit further--

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.500
and you'll actually see a
bunch of these when Dale Birtch

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.000
presents--

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.940
we offer several different
tools on Google Cloud Platform

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:43.590
to help you answer
these questions

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.690
at various levels of detail.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:50.580
So one thing that we launched
recently is billing reports.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.280
So at a glance, you can
see your cost trends

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.820
overall and within specific
products, within specific SKUs,

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.110
over specific time ranges.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.330
So you can kind of drill
down right in the billing UI

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.370
and see what your costs are.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:05.580
And then we recently launched--
and we have a team for this

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:06.714
in the room--

00:04:06.714 --> 00:04:08.880
forecasting within reports,
so you can see forecasts

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.130
based on the specific
configuration of reports

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:15.617
you're looking at to see how the
general trend looks over time.

00:04:15.617 --> 00:04:16.950
We also have budgets and alerts.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.920
Matt Leonard gave a great
talk on this with [? Lucas ?]

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.510
[? Carlson ?] from [INAUDIBLE]
yesterday on how to set up

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:25.230
budgets and alerts and receive
notifications about them,

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:28.459
either via email or
programmatically via Pub/Sub.

00:04:28.459 --> 00:04:30.000
So you could set up
a Cloud Function.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.940
You could respond to them
automatically and set up

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.342
very nice controls
in your environment.

00:04:35.342 --> 00:04:37.050
And then finally, we
have billing export.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:38.675
So this is where you
really want to get

00:04:38.675 --> 00:04:40.730
into the fine-grain
details of your bill.

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:45.510
You want to understand
what resources are running,

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:50.150
what labels are attached
to those resources,

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:52.680
whether it's the machine type
or which project the BigQuery

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.000
analysis took place in.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.320
A lot of the detail
is in BigQuery export,

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.770
and that can drive some
really nice dashboards

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:01.680
that help your teams understand
what's going on in the cloud.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.080
So before I turn
it over to Dale,

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.470
just to set the stage about
what's actually going on,

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:12.132
this shouldn't be too--

00:05:12.132 --> 00:05:14.090
I guess I'm right in the
middle of the screen--

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:16.430
let me walk over to the side.

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:18.470
So this shouldn't be
surprising, but, basically,

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:20.370
billing starts
with actual usage.

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.720
So on the far left
here, we meter the usage

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:24.930
at the product-level.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:28.730
And what happens to that usage
is it goes into something

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:30.410
I'm going to call
the pricing engine.

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:32.410
And I have some tech leads
in here who are like,

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:33.480
there is no such thing.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:34.730
That's not what we call it.

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:36.440
There are many stories way
more complicated than that.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.050
And I'm like, yes, but I'm a
product manager, so I represent

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:41.640
complexity with a black box.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.754
So usage comes into
the pricing engine,

00:05:43.754 --> 00:05:45.920
and there are a bunch of
different pricing schedules

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.730
that we marry to usage to
produce the cost of your usage.

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:51.980
This should feel right.

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:53.930
This equation should
not be surprising.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.300
And then there are several
places where these costs go.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:59.720
So first of all, all
the costs go to Dale--

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:03.440
not of all cloud customers,
just of Dale's costs,

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:04.570
and that's via the invoice.

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:07.000
And then he gets to decide,
do I like this invoice?

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:08.480
Do I want to pay this invoice?

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.127
Am I going to pay it on time?

00:06:10.127 --> 00:06:11.960
And generally, he has
been doing that, which

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.130
is why he's on stage with me.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:15.810
So the second place
that this could go

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:17.730
is cloud billing reports.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:20.370
And this is the way that you
can see your costs at a glance

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:23.567
immediately in the
billing console.

00:06:23.567 --> 00:06:25.650
And this is meant to satisfy
many different users.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:27.483
So it could be the chief
technology officer.

00:06:27.483 --> 00:06:29.250
It could be the site
reliability engineer.

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:32.160
It could be the project owner.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.120
Anyone with billing
privileges can see the costs

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:37.920
in their project and
billing accounts.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.586
But what we're going
to talk about today

00:06:39.586 --> 00:06:41.630
is the other place that
these detailed costs

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:45.450
go, which is into BigQuery
billing export if you enable it

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:49.110
and then, what you can do with
BigQuery and that billing data,

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:51.750
once you've set it up.

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:54.750
And the goal here is really
to help you measure, monitor,

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:57.580
and manage the metrics that
matter the most to you.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.190
So just keep that in mind.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:01.470
The three fundamental
questions-- what are my costs?

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:02.550
What are my cost trends?

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:03.930
What are my cost drivers?

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:06.000
And how do I set up the
queries and dashboards

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.815
that really show me what I need
to know to run my business?

00:07:09.815 --> 00:07:12.814
Without further ado, I'll let
Dale Birtch take it from here.

00:07:15.179 --> 00:07:16.470
DALE HOPKINS: That's all right.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:17.505
One of the Dales?

00:07:17.505 --> 00:07:18.630
So what I wanted to do is--

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:21.570
I guess I'd start off in that
we've been a Google Cloud

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.215
customer now for just
over eight years.

00:07:23.215 --> 00:07:24.840
And so, you'll find
out that's actually

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:27.210
before Google Cloud was
called Google Cloud.

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:28.560
We started off on App Engine.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:30.310
And App Engine had
some interesting things

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:31.890
about billing.

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:36.810
But for us, we've got about a
million small businesses that

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:38.340
currently use our software.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:41.040
And we've got 19,000
salespeople that ended up

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:43.140
using our platform to
deliver those services

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:44.440
to their customers.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:48.000
So we do have an interesting
problem in that that's actually

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.370
quite a bit of work to be done.

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:51.690
And so Google Cloud has
been an integral part

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:54.180
of our strategy in terms of
delivering that and managing

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:55.260
the scale.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:56.760
But one of the
things that you often

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.360
don't hear about the cloud
is that, while the cloud does

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:03.120
allow you to avoid
availability and scalability

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:06.300
problems of that nature,
what it doesn't do

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.440
is it doesn't eliminate
all scaling problems.

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:09.920
So one of the things
you'll find is

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:12.460
that, when you trade out when
you have your own hardware,

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:16.320
is when the load scales, instead
of you paying with availability

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:17.820
or latency or
problems such as that,

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:20.400
you end up just
paying with cost.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:22.680
And so the important part
is that, as your application

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:25.200
scales, so does your cost.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:28.264
And that can scale in ways that
you weren't 100% ready for.

00:08:28.264 --> 00:08:29.805
And so watching over
your cloud costs

00:08:29.805 --> 00:08:32.400
is a really important part,
like one of the basics.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.309
It's really important
to how you use cloud.

00:08:35.309 --> 00:08:38.378
And so that's why we've used
it in a lot of different ways.

00:08:38.378 --> 00:08:40.169
And we've used that
kind of from the start.

00:08:40.169 --> 00:08:42.002
And it's gotten a lot
better over the years.

00:08:42.002 --> 00:08:43.620
And specifically,
what we found is

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.960
that Google doesn't
miss a lot of things

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:47.464
in terms of your bill.

00:08:47.464 --> 00:08:48.630
It's actually very accurate.

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:49.714
The usage is very correct.

00:08:49.714 --> 00:08:51.963
You probably haven't found
out at the end of the month

00:08:51.963 --> 00:08:53.840
that they forgot to
charge you for something.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:55.881
And so for us, because of
that level of accuracy,

00:08:55.881 --> 00:08:58.390
it's actually a really good
diagnostic tool for us to use.

00:08:58.390 --> 00:08:59.550
So Dale Birtch is
going to walk you

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:00.758
through some of these things.

00:09:00.758 --> 00:09:03.420
And we promise to have a live
demo that you guys can actually

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:04.110
follow along.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:06.693
And we're going to build some
of the stuff from the ground up.

00:09:06.693 --> 00:09:09.095
So with that, here you go, Dale.

00:09:09.095 --> 00:09:10.470
DALE BIRTCH: Thank
you very much.

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:13.710
As he said, I'm the
other Dale from Vendasta.

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:15.900
How I got involved
with billing originally

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:20.340
was when we first started an
SRE team back in December 2016.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:22.170
Dale came to us with a problem.

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:25.271
He showed up in the room one day
and said, how are things going?

00:09:25.271 --> 00:09:26.520
And we're, like, I don't know.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:27.728
Things are going pretty good.

00:09:27.728 --> 00:09:29.550
He goes, I got this
month's Google bill,

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:32.002
and it was $20,000
higher than we expected.

00:09:32.002 --> 00:09:34.530
And like, that's
probably a problem.

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:35.700
That's a lot of beer.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:39.690
So when you equate a Google
bill to being a lot of beer,

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:42.030
it doesn't take
much to suddenly go

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:45.812
from a $60,000 bill
to an $80,000 bill,

00:09:45.812 --> 00:09:48.270
all because of a bug that went
in at the start of the month

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:49.830
and nobody noticed.

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:52.380
And App Engine just
scaled and handled that.

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:53.730
We had the additional traffic.

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:55.650
None of our customers
saw any outages.

00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:58.800
We didn't see any problems,
and we just got a big bill.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.710
So that was the first postmortem
we held as an SRE team.

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:04.080
And we sat down with the
team, and we went, OK, guys,

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:05.040
how did the bug get in?

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:06.450
How did we review it?

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:08.460
What were the steps
we took in place?

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:10.496
And how do we stop this
from happening again?

00:10:10.496 --> 00:10:12.870
And the team went, well, if
we're going to own a project,

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:15.180
we kind of also need to own
the billing for the project.

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:16.290
We need to know what it costs.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:17.706
And we need to see
that before you

00:10:17.706 --> 00:10:19.880
come to us with a postmortem
booking and saying you

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:22.230
guys spent $20,000
extra, what the hell?

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:24.841
So that's where I got
involved with this.

00:10:24.841 --> 00:10:27.090
We were trying to come up
with some type of a solution

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:29.460
to track our billing in 2016.

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:32.400
And there wasn't really
anything great available.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:35.400
We looked at crazy things like
scraping the App Engine status

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:37.500
board to give you daily totals.

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:40.320
We figured out the BigQuery
export at that point.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:42.910
And I sat down with one of
our data analysts and said,

00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:44.490
can you teach me
how to Data Studio?

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:47.080
And she kind of laughed and
went, well, even you can do it.

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:50.070
So that's how I ended
up in Data Studio.

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:51.930
And we've been using
it for dashboards

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:54.600
and the individual
team boards ever since.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:56.850
So I'm going to jump
over here into the demo

00:10:56.850 --> 00:11:00.970
and start at the
Reports tab and Billing.

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:03.810
It's important to understand
the scale of Vendasta here.

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:06.810
So we've got 96 billable
projects within the Google

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:07.540
Cloud platform--

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:11.490
34 Google products that we
get billed for and 400 SKUs.

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.830
So it is a little difficult
to keep track of which ones

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:14.788
we actually care about.

00:11:14.788 --> 00:11:16.620
Most of the projects
aren't actually

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:18.420
costing us a lot of money.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:20.482
They handle small services.

00:11:20.482 --> 00:11:22.690
And most of the SKUs don't
have a large volume on it.

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:24.480
There's probably 10
or 12 in every project

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:27.810
that I actually care about
and want to track closely.

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:31.239
So when I look at the
dashboard like this,

00:11:31.239 --> 00:11:33.030
I can try and drill
into individual things.

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:34.030
But I'm always guessing.

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:36.720
I'm guessing at what project
is having a problem, what area

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:38.520
of that project,
the product it is,

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:41.054
or what SKUs within that
product are actually driving it.

00:11:41.054 --> 00:11:42.720
And yes, I can find
a lot of information

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:43.800
from this dashboard.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:45.780
And their forecasts
that they announced

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:47.670
recently, we've been
using them for a while,

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:48.840
and they're pretty killer.

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:50.640
I can't match that
in Data Studio.

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:52.590
But to actually present
this information

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:54.330
to a team is
difficult. There's not

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:55.622
a great "I Am" story around it.

00:11:55.622 --> 00:11:57.955
I don't want to give out
permissions at the organization

00:11:57.955 --> 00:11:58.740
level for it.

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:00.750
There's no premade
reports that they can just

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.630
hit this and see the view
that they had from last time,

00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:04.637
or anything like that.

00:12:04.637 --> 00:12:06.720
So while those things are
coming, in the meantime,

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:08.640
we still have
motivation to put effort

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:11.380
into building our own set of
dashboards [INAUDIBLE] here.

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:13.290
So I'm going to start
today by building

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:15.070
one of those dashboards
from scratch.

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:17.940
So what I'm going
to do is first,

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:19.650
I'm going to set up an export.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:21.229
So we've got
billing export here.

00:12:21.229 --> 00:12:22.020
I'm going to go in.

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:23.610
And obviously, I've
already set this up.

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:25.360
We've been running it
for a few years now.

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:27.630
But it's going to say
which BigQuery project?

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:31.090
I'm going to have a set up
to export to-- in this case,

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:32.430
it's this project--

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:34.630
and what table set we're
going to have in there.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:37.410
So once this is saved, it's
going to, going forward,

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.750
save all of your billing
data to BigQuery.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:41.350
It doesn't do it retroactively.

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:43.980
But once you start, you can
start to build a set of data

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:46.290
that you can use,
then, to analyze.

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:48.360
So I'm going to jump
over to BigQuery here.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:51.390
And I'm on the data set that
I just set up to configure.

00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:54.330
Its Cloud Costs, and there are
a number of tables in there.

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:55.830
I'll explain some
of the other ones.

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:58.850
But the one we care about
is the Billing Export v1.

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:01.980
So since October
of last year when

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:04.690
billing export became
GA and everybody got it,

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:06.474
it's versioned as v1.

00:13:06.474 --> 00:13:08.640
Prior to that is the couple
of years of billing data

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:11.970
that we had before with some
different headers and whatnot.

00:13:11.970 --> 00:13:14.667
Billing export. v1
is this table set.

00:13:14.667 --> 00:13:16.500
And if I preview this
table, you can kind of

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:17.916
get a feel for
what we're actually

00:13:17.916 --> 00:13:19.510
saving to BigQuery here.

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:23.880
So for every service, for
every project, for every SKU,

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:25.630
there's a line
item for every hour

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:27.330
of what that was built up with.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:29.490
So I can see, I've got
Compute Engine, storage

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:32.790
of an image in a
project of this project,

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:36.280
labels that I've applied
to that resource--

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:40.110
so you can split resources
by custom labels.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:43.290
And then I actually get
the cost that it was--

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:47.910
so the cost and the currency
and the usage amount.

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:50.280
So having all this
data in BigQuery

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:54.450
is going to allow me to actually
build graphs and do analytics

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:55.810
over that.

00:13:55.810 --> 00:13:58.620
So how did we do this before is
probably an important question?

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:01.770
Well, I'm sure some others
have spreadsheets like this.

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:06.060
But we have these sheets
that date back to 2011.

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:08.331
And every month, we would
get the Google bill,

00:14:08.331 --> 00:14:10.080
and we would sit there
in the spreadsheet,

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:12.870
and we'd type every line
item in for every row.

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:14.850
And when we saw things
that got screwed up,

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:16.800
we were like, oh, no, we've
got to do something about that.

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:18.480
So there are lots
of examples in here

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.030
where we jumped $10,000
a month on a row item.

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:23.520
And then three months later,
we were down to $3,000

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:27.840
a month on it, where we added
$5,000 in front-end instances.

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:31.080
And it was always a struggle to
actually do anything proactive

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:34.660
with it because we were always
reacting to-- uh-oh, big bill.

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:37.030
How do we handle this one now?

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:39.400
So we wasted a lot of
beer on these resources.

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:42.420
We've made that back
significantly by doing

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:45.310
more real-time analysis on it.

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:50.129
So the next step, once we
have an export in BigQuery,

00:14:50.129 --> 00:14:51.420
is to actually use Data Studio.

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:52.860
So I'm going to
go in Data Studio,

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:56.150
and I'm going to start
a brand-new blank sheet.

00:14:56.150 --> 00:14:58.480
DALE HOPKINS: So this
is for the group here.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:01.236
How many of you are currently
using a billing export?

00:15:01.236 --> 00:15:01.760
OK.

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:02.510
DALE BIRTCH: Nice.

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:02.980
DALE HOPKINS: And
how many of you

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:05.690
are actually tying this
today to Data Studio today?

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:06.390
OK, cool.

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:07.550
DALE BIRTCH: Nice.

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:10.080
So once we open up
Data Studio here,

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.070
I'm going to give it
a new data source.

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:13.360
It's pretty straightforward.

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:15.780
It's going to ask me which
source I want to use.

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:18.420
That'll be a BigQuery
in this situation.

00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:21.090
I'm going to find my project
and table name that I set up

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:24.130
in the first place.

00:15:24.130 --> 00:15:30.160
Data set here, costs--

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:32.390
and I'm going to choose
my v1 export, which is

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:34.820
what I configured in my save.

00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:36.860
So this is going to
connect this BigQuery

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:39.381
table to my Data Studio sheets.

00:15:39.381 --> 00:15:40.880
Now, the first thing
I'm going to do

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:43.075
is-- record count I
don't really care about.

00:15:43.075 --> 00:15:44.700
I really want to do
everything by cost.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:46.700
So I'm just going to
Disable record count

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:48.720
and say, Add This to Report.

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:50.710
So now I have a
blank Data Studio

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:53.870
report that is connected
to all of my billing data.

00:15:53.870 --> 00:15:57.440
So from here, I can do something
like draw a time series graph

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:00.820
to represent what is my cost.

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:03.070
So I start a brand new graph.

00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:05.380
It's automatically going to
figure out that usage start

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:06.760
time is what I'm graphing.

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:08.650
And I can see my daily
cost in this graph

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:11.560
over the last month for all
of my App Engine projects

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:13.457
and all of my resources.

00:16:13.457 --> 00:16:15.790
From there, I probably want
to give a little more value,

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:19.150
like a scorecard, to say, what
is the totals for this period?

00:16:19.150 --> 00:16:21.330
So we're just going to
draw a scorecard here.

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:23.800
That will give me my totals
for that time period.

00:16:28.079 --> 00:16:29.620
And it's running
its BigQuery queries

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:32.078
in the background to calculate
all of that information that

00:16:32.078 --> 00:16:33.890
gets shown on the screen here.

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:37.310
It should be noted, you do get
billed for the BigQuery queries

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:38.540
and for storing the data.

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:39.440
It's super minimal.

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:40.640
I've tried to
calculate it before,

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:43.015
and we're in single dollars
a day over the last few years

00:16:43.015 --> 00:16:45.952
that we have data in there.

00:16:45.952 --> 00:16:47.660
If you were to query
something like logs,

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:48.860
it's going to be
much more expensive.

00:16:48.860 --> 00:16:50.150
I've made that mistake before.

00:16:50.150 --> 00:16:52.800
Data Studio is not the
right tool for that.

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:55.604
So now that I've got a total
[INAUDIBLE] cost on here,

00:16:55.604 --> 00:16:58.020
I probably want to give a
little more control to the user.

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:00.086
So let's put a
daterange selector on.

00:17:00.086 --> 00:17:01.210
Again, it's the same thing.

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:04.730
You just drag on a
daterange selector,

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:07.640
and you can set a
default daterange for it.

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:12.530
I usually do something along
the lines of the last 14 days.

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:15.380
So now I have a graph that shows
me my totals for the last 14

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:17.900
days and lets a
user configure that.

00:17:17.900 --> 00:17:21.680
I also want to do something like
give them a filter option so

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:24.200
that the user can
choose which projects

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:27.770
they want to do, which projects
they want to drill down into,

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:29.750
and explore some of
their own metrics.

00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:31.790
So I'm going to
put this up here.

00:17:31.790 --> 00:17:34.580
Its dimension is going to
be the project ID by cost.

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:36.500
So at this point,
I have a graph that

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:38.720
lets me choose
dateranges, shows me

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:40.610
the total for that
daterange, and lets

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:43.080
me pick an individual project.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:46.546
So I could say I just want
to see this one project.

00:17:46.546 --> 00:17:48.920
So I'm looking at a project
now, and I've got an anomaly.

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:51.010
That doesn't look
like the last 14 days.

00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:54.019
How do I actually know,
is that normal, or not?

00:17:54.019 --> 00:17:56.060
From there, we can start
to use some of the tools

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:57.500
that Data Studio provides.

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:00.470
So if I look at
any graph in here,

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:02.120
I can put a compare
on that says,

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:05.670
look at it over the previous
period and graph that as well.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:08.690
So now I've got a light blue
line and a dark blue line,

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:11.900
the light blue line being what
that range was for the last 14

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:14.780
days, and the dark
blue line being

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:17.360
what we're actually running
in this current time period.

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:21.210
So now I when I view this
graph and grab my project,

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:23.600
I can tell, is that something
that I should actually

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:24.860
care about and investigate?

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:28.040
Yes, it probably is because
it's an anomaly to what we

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:31.200
expect it to be in that period.

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:33.170
So this gives you
a really basic--

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:36.770
I have a graph now that I can
say, share it with a team.

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:40.250
We can put it on a TV in a
room and represent their one

00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:41.660
individual project.

00:18:41.660 --> 00:18:43.280
This brings in all
of the identity

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:45.720
and Access Management
of the Google Docs

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:47.810
that you could say, here,
this person can have it.

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:49.970
You can share a link
outside of the organization.

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:52.220
And you can widely
distribute those graphs

00:18:52.220 --> 00:18:55.160
and not have to be
concerned about permissions

00:18:55.160 --> 00:18:58.280
or giving access to
projects that you wouldn't

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:01.530
want to give otherwise.

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:04.680
So from here, I'm going to
look at some of the options

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:08.250
that you can do outside
of building your own.

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:11.370
So Google actually has
given a set of templates

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:14.340
for visualizing your time
spent in Data Studio.

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:16.290
They wrote this blog
post on it and link you

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:18.300
to an example report.

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:20.159
If you read through
this blog post,

00:19:20.159 --> 00:19:21.950
it basically walks you
through the same set

00:19:21.950 --> 00:19:24.570
up-- exporting your data,
looking at your data,

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:28.200
and then using this billing
report demo link, which

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:31.740
opens up this billing
report in Data Studio

00:19:31.740 --> 00:19:34.200
that you can clone
into your organization,

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:36.030
so make a copy of the report.

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:38.360
And it's going to ask you
to set up your data sources.

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:40.710
The instructions are all in
the blog on how to do that.

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:44.806
But we're going to
grab a billing export.

00:19:44.806 --> 00:19:47.530
I believe the second one
is a spending trends,

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:53.070
and the third one is a BigQuery
audit source for billing.

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:56.130
And cloning that report
then gives you something

00:19:56.130 --> 00:19:59.010
to start with that
has all of your data

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:00.750
in a prepopulated dashboard.

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:02.820
And we don't heavily
leverage this internally,

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.550
because it's broken
down mostly by product--

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:10.350
by your BigQuery, by your App
Engine, by your cloud costs--

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:12.900
and we tend to break
things down by project

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:15.000
because teams are
responsible for projects, not

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:18.090
for individual products across
the whole Google Platform.

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:20.130
But it's a great
spot to actually grab

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:23.380
this visualization.

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:24.630
You can copy graphs out of it.

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:29.020
You can look at exactly how
they have designed the graphs

00:20:29.020 --> 00:20:32.220
and what data powers it
and grab ideas from there

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:34.470
to populate your own
custom dashboards that

00:20:34.470 --> 00:20:37.470
would be more useful in your
organization or your type

00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:38.600
of setup here.

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:43.320
So I can basically see,
by project, my spending

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:46.882
by day, and over time in here.

00:20:46.882 --> 00:20:48.340
So I can jump to
Projects, and it's

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:52.290
got a bunch of nice bar
graphs and pie graphs

00:20:52.290 --> 00:20:57.354
around how we're
actually spending money

00:20:57.354 --> 00:20:58.270
once it comes up here.

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:05.240
So this is a great source
for finding information

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:08.930
and figuring out how to
actually build dashboards

00:21:08.930 --> 00:21:10.700
within Data Studio.

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:14.030
We built our own from scratch,
initially, to start with.

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:16.250
We find that we can
get a more custom view.

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:19.040
You can tailor something
specific to a team

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:22.530
and give that to them, and then
they can own that information.

00:21:22.530 --> 00:21:24.380
So I'm going to jump
over another tab

00:21:24.380 --> 00:21:26.810
and actually look at
what we have built.

00:21:26.810 --> 00:21:32.300
So we've got 20 pages in
this Data Studio sheet

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:34.950
that are different reports
for different purposes.

00:21:34.950 --> 00:21:36.770
So we've got a total
cost report that

00:21:36.770 --> 00:21:38.510
breaks down our
costs by environment

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:40.700
and shows it compared
to the last two weeks

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:43.820
with our cost and our credits.

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:46.100
This is on a TV in
our atrium where

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:48.080
every person in the
organization sees it

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:50.030
so that anybody walking
can be empowered

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:52.269
to go, what happened there,
and ask that question.

00:21:52.269 --> 00:21:53.810
And we want to
encourage teams to ask

00:21:53.810 --> 00:21:56.750
that question because then you
take ownership of the project.

00:21:56.750 --> 00:22:00.350
You can make those changes
to really drive down costs

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:03.510
and manage them over time.

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:07.100
So from there, we've
got individual projects

00:22:07.100 --> 00:22:10.250
and also showing the
same type of overlays

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:14.040
with the last periods of time.

00:22:14.040 --> 00:22:16.100
So if we looked at
something like data storage

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:18.770
rates and storage, we have
spikes every week here.

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:21.680
And having the context of what
it was over the last period

00:22:21.680 --> 00:22:23.840
tells me that those spikes
are probably normal.

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:25.497
We do bulk work at that point.

00:22:25.497 --> 00:22:27.080
But if I just saw
the spike otherwise,

00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:28.910
I might spend some
time investigating,

00:22:28.910 --> 00:22:31.970
similar to this Google Cloud
Endpoints or service control

00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:36.046
line item that was $150
higher than expected that day.

00:22:36.046 --> 00:22:37.670
That's probably worth
me investigating.

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:39.170
And sure enough,
there was a Pub/Sub

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:41.510
that was failing and
causing that to retry

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:43.590
and additional traffic.

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:45.440
But I probably don't
want to actually look

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:48.800
at the extra $150 we spent on
data store reads and writes

00:22:48.800 --> 00:22:52.520
in that period, because I
can see that they're normal.

00:22:52.520 --> 00:22:56.540
That gives me the context to
look at our data over time.

00:22:56.540 --> 00:22:58.370
I use a lot of
this data as an SRE

00:22:58.370 --> 00:23:01.050
to see what's happening
in our organization.

00:23:01.050 --> 00:23:03.680
So for example, our Cloud
SQL costs jumped here.

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:05.180
That probably means,
to me, somebody

00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:07.520
added a new Cloud SQL
database or changed the way

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:08.240
it was scaling.

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:09.560
And if they haven't
talked to me about that,

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:11.630
I'm probably concerned
about how they set up

00:23:11.630 --> 00:23:13.430
security on that
Cloud SQL database

00:23:13.430 --> 00:23:16.340
and what they're
actually using that for.

00:23:16.340 --> 00:23:19.580
Maybe it's a Dev database
that they provisioned

00:23:19.580 --> 00:23:21.140
with production resources.

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:22.490
It's $5 a day.

00:23:22.490 --> 00:23:25.550
That's not a huge number, but
if it can be a smaller instance

00:23:25.550 --> 00:23:27.170
type, we'll move that down.

00:23:27.170 --> 00:23:29.870
So generally, when I'm looking
at Data Studio reports,

00:23:29.870 --> 00:23:32.750
there are two different things
I'm looking for, either slow

00:23:32.750 --> 00:23:35.400
burn-- things that are
eating up cost over time

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:37.100
and just very, very
slowly creeping,

00:23:37.100 --> 00:23:39.886
or sudden changes
within our billing data

00:23:39.886 --> 00:23:41.510
where something spiked
and we're trying

00:23:41.510 --> 00:23:45.920
to catch it before it grows
and goes out of control.

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:49.070
If I look through some examples
that we've had in the past year

00:23:49.070 --> 00:23:50.650
or so--

00:23:50.650 --> 00:23:52.940
I had talked about
the data store

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:56.590
reads matching almost exactly
with the previous period.

00:23:56.590 --> 00:23:57.850
That's not a concern.

00:23:57.850 --> 00:24:01.970
Cloud Endpoints spiking, that
is more of a concern for me.

00:24:01.970 --> 00:24:04.595
If I go to something
like this graph--

00:24:04.595 --> 00:24:07.940
this is an external
blog that we host.

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:11.240
And it also hosts some
of our internal KPIs

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:13.010
that get run against
BigQuery every day

00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:15.060
and say how many
reports did we make?

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:16.784
How many customers signed--

00:24:16.784 --> 00:24:17.700
those types of things.

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:20.360
And that's a BigQuery job that
happens under Cloud Tasks.

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:23.490
And in this case,
February 13 of this year,

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:25.950
the query to BigQuery
was successful,

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:27.650
but the task wasn't completing.

00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:29.487
So the task was failing
and then retrying.

00:24:29.487 --> 00:24:31.820
And the next hour, we would
try and collect stats again.

00:24:31.820 --> 00:24:34.899
It would still call out to
BigQuery and fail and retry.

00:24:34.899 --> 00:24:36.440
And we get a graph
that kind of looks

00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:40.220
like this where, over the course
of a weekend, in a few days,

00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:42.440
we went from spending
like $15 a day

00:24:42.440 --> 00:24:45.290
on analysis to spending $325.

00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:47.780
And having a billing report
in front of a team-- somebody

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:51.260
looked at it, and went, oh,
yeah, we should go fix that.

00:24:51.260 --> 00:24:53.780
Because that failing task isn't
just a single failing task.

00:24:53.780 --> 00:24:57.750
It's actually costing us
hundreds of dollars a day.

00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:01.490
Similarly, this is
the same project.

00:25:01.490 --> 00:25:03.980
We had just started doing
some development on it,

00:25:03.980 --> 00:25:05.630
and it is one of
our few projects

00:25:05.630 --> 00:25:07.400
that uses App Engine Flex.

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:09.200
And you have to spin
up your own machines

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:10.886
and spin down your own machines.

00:25:10.886 --> 00:25:12.260
It doesn't just
take care of that

00:25:12.260 --> 00:25:14.900
when you move traffic around.

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:18.230
So in this case, they were
deploying new versions

00:25:18.230 --> 00:25:20.870
and failing to spin
down the old versions.

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:24.440
So after a few days, we realized
that the cost in that project

00:25:24.440 --> 00:25:25.850
was climbing, and
we were getting

00:25:25.850 --> 00:25:29.720
billed for more flex instance
hours and flex RAM costs

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:31.584
that we were expecting to.

00:25:31.584 --> 00:25:33.000
Just have that
chat with the team.

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:34.310
Hey, you guys are
spending more money

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:35.559
than you're expecting to here.

00:25:35.559 --> 00:25:36.349
What's going on?

00:25:36.349 --> 00:25:38.390
These are the line items
that you are using here.

00:25:38.390 --> 00:25:40.334
DALE HOPKINS: We've
had lots of examples.

00:25:40.334 --> 00:25:41.750
You guys have
probably heard this.

00:25:41.750 --> 00:25:43.220
We had a development team.

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:45.180
And what they wanted to do was
some load testing on a service,

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:46.850
so they spun up the
biggest possible VM

00:25:46.850 --> 00:25:48.174
they could find on GCP.

00:25:48.174 --> 00:25:49.340
They ran all of their tests.

00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:50.960
And then one of them
forgot to spin it down

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:51.910
at the end of the day.

00:25:51.910 --> 00:25:54.410
And it doesn't take long before
you spin something like that

00:25:54.410 --> 00:25:56.780
up, and the, all of a
sudden, your test projects,

00:25:56.780 --> 00:25:58.907
you're seeing this
shark fin like this.

00:25:58.907 --> 00:26:00.990
And this is how Dale ends
up finding these things.

00:26:00.990 --> 00:26:02.840
And so it's actually really
helpful because a lot

00:26:02.840 --> 00:26:04.040
of the stuff that,
at our office,

00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:05.720
we're pretty open with
permissions on developers,

00:26:05.720 --> 00:26:06.886
so they can create machines.

00:26:06.886 --> 00:26:08.870
They can do lots of stuff.

00:26:08.870 --> 00:26:10.820
And this kind of helps
catch those anomalies,

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:13.380
that, it's, like, not
part of normal operations.

00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:16.250
DALE BIRTCH: Yeah, and another
one that's super common is slow

00:26:16.250 --> 00:26:20.450
burn-- so where something
just creeps up over time, over

00:26:20.450 --> 00:26:22.160
and over again, and
you don't really

00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:26.120
notice that it's costing
you a whole bunch of money.

00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:29.700
If I look at a project here--

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:31.600
which one am I in?

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:33.910
Our BigQuery costs for storage--

00:26:33.910 --> 00:26:36.410
if you look at our long-term
storage costs here in the green

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:40.496
line, it went up by basically
$0.50 a day and had been

00:26:40.496 --> 00:26:41.620
climbing for quite a while.

00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:43.940
And we realized that
bad retention policies

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:45.740
happen all the time.

00:26:45.740 --> 00:26:47.780
When you create a retention
policy in BigQuery,

00:26:47.780 --> 00:26:50.180
it's only on newly-created
tables after that.

00:26:50.180 --> 00:26:52.220
It doesn't take effect
to all the old tables.

00:26:52.220 --> 00:26:54.595
So we had terabytes
of data hanging around

00:26:54.595 --> 00:26:56.470
in BigQuery that we
really didn't care about.

00:26:56.470 --> 00:26:59.150
But because we didn't make
the buckets with a retention

00:26:59.150 --> 00:27:02.090
policy, they were just hanging
around forever and ever.

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:05.690
And we sat down and well,
this just keeps increasing.

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:07.430
We have to do
something about that.

00:27:07.430 --> 00:27:10.140
It's not increasing in
value to the organization.

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:13.340
So let's write some scripts,
actually hit BigQuery,

00:27:13.340 --> 00:27:15.780
and expire all those tables,
set expiry times on them.

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:19.670
And once they expire, the
cost drops back down again.

00:27:19.670 --> 00:27:22.787
We've seen it in projects where
our cloud storage cost will

00:27:22.787 --> 00:27:24.620
creep up over time, and
next thing you know,

00:27:24.620 --> 00:27:26.520
we're spending $30 a
day in cloud storage.

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:29.222
Well, we did backups every
single night for multiple years

00:27:29.222 --> 00:27:31.430
and never cleaned up a
backup, because there was just

00:27:31.430 --> 00:27:34.250
no retention policy
on those buckets.

00:27:34.250 --> 00:27:36.020
Another favorite of
mine that we'll find,

00:27:36.020 --> 00:27:40.280
looking into individual line
items, is unused IP addresses.

00:27:40.280 --> 00:27:42.950
You don't pay for static IPs
in Google's infrastructure

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:45.170
unless you don't have them
assigned to something.

00:27:45.170 --> 00:27:46.710
And then you start
paying for them.

00:27:46.710 --> 00:27:49.280
So it's a burden to
spend-- it's not expensive.

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:50.197
It's $5 or $6 a month.

00:27:50.197 --> 00:27:52.238
But when you have 30 or
40 of them hanging around

00:27:52.238 --> 00:27:54.010
because somebody
deprovisioned something,

00:27:54.010 --> 00:27:55.464
it adds up over time.

00:27:55.464 --> 00:27:58.130
And that's beer that we could be
drinking instead of spending it

00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:01.570
on static IP addresses
we're just not using.

00:28:01.570 --> 00:28:06.230
So I tend to use our cloud costs
as a set of metrics over what's

00:28:06.230 --> 00:28:07.490
going on in my environment.

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:11.030
Newly created items, workloads
that I'm not expecting,

00:28:11.030 --> 00:28:13.045
sudden changes in cost--

00:28:13.045 --> 00:28:14.420
they're usually
tied to something

00:28:14.420 --> 00:28:16.970
a development team is doing and
gives me that resource that I

00:28:16.970 --> 00:28:18.990
can go talk to the
development team and say,

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:20.640
hey guys, what's going on here?

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:23.072
And it also brings that
transparency to the teams,

00:28:23.072 --> 00:28:24.530
that, they can own
their own costs.

00:28:24.530 --> 00:28:26.450
They can look at their
graphs over time.

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:28.190
They can be responsible
for those things.

00:28:28.190 --> 00:28:30.890
And it kind of pushes that
this is their project, not

00:28:30.890 --> 00:28:32.874
something that we're
administrating and telling

00:28:32.874 --> 00:28:33.540
them to work on.

00:28:36.062 --> 00:28:37.520
DALE HOPKINS: The
permissions thing

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:38.870
is also, like, it's just huge.

00:28:38.870 --> 00:28:40.730
Because you don't
necessarily want

00:28:40.730 --> 00:28:43.197
to provide access to all this
billing data to everybody.

00:28:43.197 --> 00:28:44.780
But you can provide
these great views.

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:46.279
And Dale mentioned
that you can even

00:28:46.279 --> 00:28:47.480
do these things externally.

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:49.010
So if you want to share
these with somebody else,

00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:51.110
the permission model
is just a huge deal.

00:28:51.110 --> 00:28:54.710
And for everybody in here,
the sooner you can give people

00:28:54.710 --> 00:28:56.160
telemetry on what
they're doing--

00:28:56.160 --> 00:28:58.530
I mean, you don't even have
to tell developers that they

00:28:58.530 --> 00:28:59.780
should be worried about costs.

00:28:59.780 --> 00:29:01.340
If they can actually
see the costs--

00:29:01.340 --> 00:29:03.200
and, I mean, they
see the shark fin.

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:05.783
A lot of the time, you're going
to find developers take action

00:29:05.783 --> 00:29:08.210
on their own stuff if
they know about this.

00:29:08.210 --> 00:29:10.430
And so, in a lot of ways,
what this is all about

00:29:10.430 --> 00:29:12.230
is just bringing
that data to people.

00:29:12.230 --> 00:29:14.720
Because, in a lot of ways,
people do a lot of bad things

00:29:14.720 --> 00:29:16.470
because they just
didn't realize it.

00:29:16.470 --> 00:29:18.800
And so it, basically, it's
producing more safety,

00:29:18.800 --> 00:29:20.510
in terms of, like, when you
do things at the office,

00:29:20.510 --> 00:29:21.890
and you know that you're
going to see the cost

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:24.401
change if you did something
wrong, it feels a lot safer.

00:29:24.401 --> 00:29:26.150
People are going to
push more comfortably.

00:29:26.150 --> 00:29:28.987
And that sort of thing is
really important to us.

00:29:28.987 --> 00:29:31.070
DALE BIRTCH: One of the
points I want to also make

00:29:31.070 --> 00:29:34.490
is with regards to the way
that the data is stored.

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:37.070
Every project
ingests billing data

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:39.000
and puts it to BigQuery
slightly differently.

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:42.550
So in Lotus, if I look at
one of my production graphs,

00:29:42.550 --> 00:29:45.740
the last day of most of our App
Engine costs all kind of drop

00:29:45.740 --> 00:29:47.490
off here.

00:29:47.490 --> 00:29:51.410
The App Engine doesn't actually
have a point yet for this day.

00:29:51.410 --> 00:29:54.860
App Engine's typically about
two days before it ends up

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:57.350
in our billing export.

00:29:57.350 --> 00:29:58.790
So there is a lag time.

00:29:58.790 --> 00:30:00.541
The same lag time is
there in the reports.

00:30:00.541 --> 00:30:02.373
So if you're doing
forecasting or something,

00:30:02.373 --> 00:30:03.920
you kind of have to
change your dates

00:30:03.920 --> 00:30:06.350
back to being a full set of
data that you're not having

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:08.210
that zero point at the end.

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:10.730
Any of these graphs, I
can go and edit and put

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:14.720
a linear regression line
across it within Data Studio

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:18.139
here and say, here's my
trend line that's linear

00:30:18.139 --> 00:30:19.430
and then say, view this report.

00:30:19.430 --> 00:30:21.230
Well, it's always going
to be trending down

00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:23.386
if I have that zero
data point at the end.

00:30:23.386 --> 00:30:25.010
The guys that are
doing the forecasting

00:30:25.010 --> 00:30:27.140
have compensated
for that and handle

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:28.640
it much better
than we can in Data

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:30.390
Studio with a simple
linear progression.

00:30:30.390 --> 00:30:33.470
So if you have the opportunity
to use the forecasting tools,

00:30:33.470 --> 00:30:35.210
they are better than
what you'll probably

00:30:35.210 --> 00:30:36.168
build yourself in here.

00:30:36.168 --> 00:30:38.000
But this can give you
a pretty basic view

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:40.550
on what you have been doing
over the course of a month.

00:30:42.605 --> 00:30:43.730
STEPHEN ELLIOTT: All right.

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:46.490
Well, thank you very much for
attending the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:30:46.490 --> 00:30:49.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

